COMPUTE LEDGER - Issue 35 - Wednesday, March 4, 2026

Three things this week.

1. Aurelian cuts Sable-3 API prices by 40%, a week after launch

Input tokens on the large model drop to $1.80 per million, output to $9. Aurelian said the cut reflects "serving efficiencies," which is what you say when a competitor is about to launch. The two other frontier labs have not matched yet; one of them told a customer on the record that it will "not compete on list price," which is a sentence that ages badly. For application companies the arithmetic is simple: if your model spend is more than a fifth of COGS, this cut is worth a re-quote this week.

2. Halyard Cloud files its S-1

The neocloud reports $1.4B in trailing revenue, 71% of it from three customers, and $6B in committed purchase obligations for accelerators it has not yet received. The filing is a good read if you want to understand how the capacity you rent is financed: mostly debt, secured by the chips, with the debt covenants tied to utilization. When utilization drops, the incentives get strange.

3. Everyone has a batch tier now

All three frontier labs and the two largest hosting providers offer a 50% discount for asynchronous requests with a 24-hour completion window. Our survey of forty production teams found that about 30% of their token volume could move to batch with no product change, and almost none of it has. The savings are real; the engineering ticket to route the traffic keeps losing to the next feature.

That is the issue. Reply with tips; we read everything.

Compute Ledger is written by two people in Oakland and supported by readers. Forward it to someone who runs a GPU budget.
